{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the border shall turn about for you from the south of Maaleh Akrabbim.</b> Wherever 'וְנָסַב' or 'וְיָצָא' is said, it teaches that the boundary was not straight but went projecting outward — the boundary goes out and turns toward the north of the world, in a diagonal to the west, and the boundary passes along the south side of Maaleh Akrabbim; thus Maaleh Akrabbim is inside the boundary." "<b>And it shall pass along to Tzinah.</b> To Tzin, like מִצְרַיְמָה." "<b>And its extremities shall be.</b> Its ends — along the south side of Kadesh Barnea." "<b>And it shall go out to Hazar Addar.</b> The boundary stretches and widens toward the north of the world and continues further in a diagonal to the west, and reaches Hazar Addar, and from there to Atzmon, and from there to the Brook of Egypt. The term 'וְנָסַב' said here — because it wrote 'וְיָצָא חֲצַר אַדָּר,' meaning it began to widen from past Kadesh Barnea, and the width of that strip that protruded toward the north was from Kadesh Barnea to Atzmon, and from there onward the boundary narrowed and turned toward the south and reached the Brook of Egypt, and from there westward to the Great Sea, which is the western boundary of all the land of Israel. Thus the Brook of Egypt is at the southwestern corner."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:45.609345194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}